Description: Fix remove ordering: restore before releasing ports, refuse late callbacks
 Upstream released the four port regions in .remove(), which the driver
 core runs before devres unregisters the devm hwmon device (dd.c 1344 vs.
 1349/610). Between them hwmon callbacks still ran against unreserved
 ports, and a write waiting on the lock during the restore could put a
 channel back into manual mode after it had been restored (B4, C4, C7,
 C8, E1; C5 only formally met).
 .
 Now:
 - ports via devm_request_region();
 - the restore action is registered with devm_add_action_or_reset()
   before devm_hwmon_device_register_with_info(), so devres tears down in
   the order debugfs, hwmon (kernfs drains running callbacks), restore,
   data, regions; .remove() is gone;
 - hand-back (pwmN_enable=2 and the unbind/unload restore) goes through
   n5_handback_locked(): CPU, SSD and HDD get their EC auto command
   (0x21, 0x2e, 0x2b) as before; PCIe (pwm4, DCR5) gets duty 255 (curve
   points 0x33-0x39 + apply 0x30) instead of 0x31. The EC has no automatic
   for DCR5: after 0x31 it kept the last written duty (befunde BEFUNDE.md,
   DCR5 0x8c), which can be 0. Same value as .shutdown (0006); operator
   decision 2 of 2026-10-16 (audit-20261016 F-2);
 - pwm4_enable=2 keeps pwm_touched for PCIe (cache pwm4 = 255, pwm4_enable
   = 2), so the unbind restore and .shutdown (0006) write 255 again. Before,
   enable=2 cleared pwm_touched and .shutdown skipped the channel, so its
   255 guarantee did not hold after a hand-back (audit-20261016 F-1);
 - the restore sets data->dead under data->lock at its start, i.e. after
   the debugfs and hwmon entries are gone. Until then callbacks run as
   usual against the still reserved ports, and the removal of those
   entries waits for running ones. n5_read/n5_write and the debugfs reader
   return -ENODEV once dead is set, so no port access can follow the
   restore;
 - a successful restore sets the cached pwm_enable to 2 and clears
   pwm_touched;
 - a failed restore is remembered per channel in a module-level mask; a
   later bind (sysfs unbind/bind, same module instance) marks that channel
   touched and "mode unknown": pwmN/pwmN_enable read -ENODATA until
   pwmN_enable is written, and the next unbind retries the restore. After
   a module reload the mask is lost (documented residual; the failed
   restore is in dmesg);
 - a successful probe does no port I/O (A5). Error path only: if the
   probe fails after the restore action is registered (hwmon or devres
   allocation), the action runs and hands back the channels an earlier
   binding left unrestored - EC automatic, PCIe 255, nothing else;
 - the pwm cache is read under data->lock (C3 hardening).
 .
 Resolves B4, C4, C7, C8 (code path), E1 and C5 (now effective);
 audit-20261016 F-1 and F-2 (with 0006).
 .
 Tested: build/static analysis as 0001. Not tested on hardware: F8a
 load/unload and unbind/bind under parallel reads/writes, kill -9 of the
 regulator, restore visible in DCR (0003) after unload, DCR5 = 0xff after
 pwm4_enable=2 and after unload.
